1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,843
Standby: if this person is out, what stalls?

2
00:00:03,843 --> 00:00:09,025
Pick any real teammate on the site and the days they will be away

3
00:00:09,025 --> 00:00:16,458
Standby walks their Teamwork Graph, then the graphs of colleagues around them, as you

4
00:00:16,458 --> 00:00:20,876
Work that also sits in a colleague's graph counts as cover: red stalls, amber thin, green covered

5
00:00:20,876 --> 00:00:26,638
Hover any node to see the work behind it

6
00:00:26,638 --> 00:00:30,258
Backups come from people who already work in the same projects, spaces and teams

7
00:00:30,258 --> 00:00:32,837
Click a count to focus on what stalls

8
00:00:32,837 --> 00:00:38,350
Each card says why it is urgent and who can pick it up

9
00:00:38,350 --> 00:00:43,062
Reassigning is opt-in per issue, and nothing changes without a confirmation

10
00:00:43,062 --> 00:00:47,780
Teamwork Graph found the shared-work links; each source reports its own status

11
00:00:47,780 --> 00:00:59,785
One confirmation drafts the handover page in Confluence

12
00:00:59,785 --> 00:01:16,532
The draft lists every item with its suggested backup, ready to edit and publish

13
00:01:16,532 --> 00:01:20,077
Standby: a bus-factor fire drill built on Teamwork Graph
